items.workedExampleMarkdown:
A quantity field on the bakery order form arrives as a string, because form values always do.

```js
const raw = '0';            // the customer typed 0

if (raw) {
  // runs: the string '0' is truthy, because only the EMPTY string is falsy
}

const quantity = Number(raw); // 0

if (quantity) {
  // does NOT run: the number 0 is falsy
}

if (quantity > 0) {
  // says what it means, and behaves the same next year
}
```

The two `if` statements above disagree about the same customer input, and neither of them is asking the question the feature actually cares about.

Equality shows the same split:

```js
raw == quantity;   // true  — '0' is converted to 0 before comparing
raw === quantity;  // false — a string is never strictly equal to a number

const shipped = null;
shipped == undefined;   // true  — the one useful loose comparison
shipped === undefined;  // false — null and undefined are distinct values
```

Write the conversion where you can see it — `Number(raw)`, `String(id)` — and then compare with `===`. The bug you avoid is the one where a value silently changed type three functions ago.

Postage bands for the fictional Larkspur Bakery mail-order service:

```js
function band(grams) {
  if (grams < 0) return 'invalid';
  if (grams < 100) return 'letter';
  if (grams < 1000) return 'parcel';
  return 'freight';
}
```

Write the boundaries down before trusting it:

| input | branch | why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `-1` | invalid | the only branch that accepts negatives |
| `0` | letter | 0 is not negative, and it is below 100 |
| `99` | letter | last accepted value of the band |
| `100` | parcel | first value the letter band rejects |
| `999` | parcel | last accepted value |
| `1000` | freight | first value that falls through the chain |

Six inputs, and every boundary in the function is pinned. Compare that with testing `50`, `500` and `5000`, which all pass whether or not a single `<` should have been `<=`.

The same discipline catches loop errors:

```js
const items = ['a', 'b', 'c'];

for (let i = 0; i <= items.length; i += 1) {
  console.log(items[i]);   // a, b, c, undefined  <- one too many
}

for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i += 1) {
  console.log(items[i]);   // a, b, c
}
```

Two versions of the same helper.

```js
// Impure: the answer depends on a value the caller cannot see or control.
let taxRate = 0.2;

function totalWithTax(subtotal) {
  return subtotal * (1 + taxRate);
}
```

```js
// Pure: everything that affects the answer is in the argument list.
function totalWithTax(subtotal, taxRate) {
  return subtotal * (1 + taxRate);
}
```

The second version can be tested with one line and no arrangement. The first cannot: a test must set `taxRate` first, must reset it afterwards, and will fail mysteriously if another test forgets. Worse, two calls with identical arguments can return different numbers, which makes any bug report about it unreproducible.

Scope, meanwhile, decides what those bodies can even see:

```js
const handlers = [];

for (var i = 0; i < 3; i += 1) {
  handlers.push(() => i);
}
handlers.map((h) => h());  // [3, 3, 3] — one shared `var` binding

const better = [];
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j += 1) {
  better.push(() => j);
}
better.map((h) => h());    // [0, 1, 2] — a fresh `let` binding per iteration
```

The callbacks were created identically. The only difference is how many bindings existed.

